The Micronix cold boot set, off disks/floppies/Micronix1.6/cold_boot_loader.bin

    cpmls -f morrow5 cold_boot_loader.bin
    cpmcp -f morrow5 cold_boot_loader.bin 0:M5LOAD ./M5LOAD

with the diskdefs here in the current directory - cpmtools reads
./diskdefs before /etc/cpmtools/diskdefs, so nothing needs installing.
The definition is the geometry we arrived at for these images: 80 tracks
of ten 512 byte sectors, 2048 byte blocks, four reserved tracks, which
puts the CP/M directory at 20480 - the same forty blocks the Micronix
floppies reserve.

There is a matched pair per controller, and it is the same two stage
arrangement as everywhere else in this system - a small loader that knows
the hardware, and a bigger one that knows the v6 filesystem:

    DJBOOT.COM   DJLOAD     floppy, DJ/DMA
    HDBOOT.COM   HDLOAD     HDCA
    M5BOOT.COM   M5LOAD     HD/DMA, 5 meg
    M10BOOT.COM  M10LOAD    HD/DMA, 10 meg
    M16BOOT.COM  M16LOAD    HD/DMA, 16 meg

M5LOAD says what it is in plain text - "Micronix loader for the HD-DMA" -
along with "Drive not ready", "Read error", "Block out of range",
"Loading" and "Entering", which are the same messages the floppy's second
level prints.  The three sizes match the three install scripts in
filesystem: m5init, m10init, m16init, and hdinit for the HDCA.

These are damaged in the archive.  The real content of M5LOAD is bytes
2048 through 7167 and the rest is E5 filler, and DJLOAD only has content
from 8192 to 9215.  E5 is what an unwritten CP/M block reads as, so these
sectors did not come off the original diskette.  The floppy's own
reserved tracks are intact, which is where micronix16-loader.bin came
from - use that one to read the second level, not DJLOAD.
